\details{
\strong{Method}

With \eqn{Z} the normalized table (samples in rows), \eqn{J} the centering
matrix, and \eqn{D} the diagonal matrix of the features' standard
deviations (or the identity matrix when \code{scale = FALSE}), PCA is the
singular value decomposition \eqn{JZD^{-1} = USV^T}. The scores are
\eqn{US}, the loadings \eqn{V}, and each axis's variance (\code{eig}) is
\eqn{s^2 / (n - 1)}, for \eqn{n} samples.

\code{prcomp()} builds the whole centered table and decomposes it in full.
\code{pca()} finds only the first \code{k} axes, by block Lanczos iteration, which
only needs products of the table with a few vectors at a time. Those are
computed in parallel from the non-zero counts alone: the zeros of each
sample all share one value after normalization (0, or
\eqn{\log(pseudocount)} minus the sample's mean log for \code{norm = 'clr'}),
and centering only subtracts each feature's mean, so a sparse table is
never made dense.

\strong{Relation to \code{pcoa()}}

The scores are the same as those of \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}} on the Euclidean distances
between the normalized samples: \code{pca(counts)} and
\code{pcoa(euclidean(counts))}, or \code{pca(counts, norm = 'clr')} and
\code{pcoa(aitchison(counts))}, give the same coordinates. PCoA's eigenvalues
are \eqn{s^2}, so \code{pca()}'s \code{eig} is \code{pcoa()}'s divided by \eqn{n - 1};
\code{prop} is the same for both. What \code{pca()} adds is the \code{loadings}: how much
each feature contributes to each axis, e.g. for a biplot.

\strong{Robust Aitchison PCA}

\code{norm = 'rclr'} with \code{pca()} is a plain PCA of the rclr-transformed table,
with its zeros left as 0. DEICODE and gemelli's Robust Aitchison PCA
instead treat those zeros as missing values and fill them in by low-rank
matrix completion (OptSpace) before the decomposition, so their results
differ.

\strong{Axis signs and reproducibility}

A principal axis's sign is arbitrary, so different software can return
mirror images of the same axis. As \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}} does, \code{pca()} flips each axis
so that its largest score in absolute value is positive, and flips its
loadings to match. Results are identical from run to run and for any
number of \code{cpus}.

\code{k} larger than the number of samples minus one, or the number of
features, is reduced to the smaller of those. Axes with variances at
rounding-error level, below \code{sqrt(.Machine$double.eps)} times the first
one, are dropped with a warning. With \code{scale = TRUE}, features with
constant values (such as features absent from every sample) are left out,
where \code{prcomp()} would stop with an error.
}
